  "repeats": [],
  "notes": [
   "Parts are the same as step 1; no changes.",
   "Breadboard orientation: the catalogue puts rows a-e in the top half ('t') and f-j in the bottom half ('b'), but the image labels the top half j-f and the bottom half e-a. I used bank 't' for the image's top half and 'b' for the bottom half. I mapped row letters top to bottom as a..e (top half) and f..j (bottom half). Only the strip (column and bank) matters electrically.",
   "Rails: the top rail's upper row is the negative rail (black line) and its lower row is the positive rail (red line). Bottom rails use the same layout. The exact hole numbers along each rail are approximate; each rail is one strip.",
   "The pushbutton straddles the centre gap in columns 5 and 7. Its two legs in the same column are the joined pair inside, so side 1 (column 5) is on 5V and side 2 (column 7) goes to pin 2 and the 10K pull-down.",
   "Schematic: 5V -> FSR -> junction -> A0 (or A1) and 10K -> GND for each sensor. 5V -> button -> pin 2 and 10K -> GND for the button.",
   "Each FSR's two legs plug directly into the breadboard: one into the sensor column and the other into the + rail. FSR legs are symmetric."
  ]
 },
